{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '61,117p'\"","aggregated_output":"    61\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty and scope.}\n    62\tBounds for independent bounded observations \\citep{Hoeffding1963} and variance-sensitive empirical bounds \\citep{Maurer2009} motivate internal safeguards in the sampled fallback. Their presence does not establish observed acceptance coverage. Bootstrap resampling \\citep{Efron1979} supports conditional uncertainty over query instances, not an extrapolation over new populations. ACS-based tasks \\citep{Ding2021} provide a public income setting; German Credit supplies a smaller credit setting \\citep{GermanCredit}. Logistic regression \\citep{Hosmer2013} and random forests \\citep{Breiman2001} expose structures useful for this computational comparison. Tabular neural architectures \\citep{Gorishniy2021} lie outside its measured model coverage.\n    63\t\n    64\t\\section{Method}\n    65\t\\subsection{Fixed-Game Target and Reliability Criteria}\n    66\tLet $f:\\mathbb{R}^d\\rightarrow[0,1]$ be a frozen classifier's positive-class probability, $x$ a query, and $Z=(z_1,\\ldots,z_B)$ a fixed background. A coalition $S\\subseteq D=\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ selects query coordinates and replaces all others with coordinates from one background row:\n    67\t\\begin{align}\n    68\t v_x(S)&=\\frac{1}{B}\\sum_{b=1}^B f(x_S,z_{b,D\\setminus S}), \\\\\n    69\t \\phi_j&=\\sum_{S\\subseteq D\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\frac{|S|!\\,(d-|S|-1)!}{d!}\n    70\t       \\big[v_x(S\\cup\\{j\\})-v_x(S)\\big]. \\label{eq:shapley}\n    71\t\\end{align}\n    72\tThis is an empirical replacement game, not a conditional-distribution estimator. Each supplied scalar-encoded original feature is a player. The probability scale and background remain identical across compared computations. In particular, logistic coefficients on the log-odds scale are not substituted for probability-scale attributions.\n    73\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L11-L64]]\n    74\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L796]]\n    75\t\n    76\tFor an estimate $\\widehat\\phi$ and recorded reference $\\phi^{\\rm ref}$, we use normalized L1 reference agreement\n    77\t\\begin{equation}\n    78\t A(\\widehat\\phi,\\phi^{\\rm ref})=\n    79\t \\left(1+\\frac{\\norm{\\widehat\\phi-\\phi^{\\rm ref}}}\n    80\t {\\max(\\norm{\\phi^{\\rm ref}},10^{-3})}\\right)^{-1}. \\label{eq:agreement}\n    81\t\\end{equation}\n    82\tThe floor prevents division by a near-zero reference norm; the score is not a probability of explanation validity. Let $T(a)$ be the indices of the three largest $|a_j|$, with original coordinate order resolving ties. Reference top-three overlap is $|T(\\widehat\\phi)\\cap T(\\phi^{\\rm ref})|/\\min(3,d)$. Sign agreement is the fraction of coordinates with equal signs, treating magnitudes at most $10^{-8}$ as zero. We also report endpoint residual\n    83\t\\begin{equation}\n    84\t E=\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{|\\sum_j\\widehat\\phi_j-[f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)]|}\n    85\t {\\max(|f(x)-v_x(\\varnothing)|,10^{-3})}\\right).\n    86\t\\end{equation}\n    87\tFor each pair of repeated calls, we compute the same top-three and sign agreements between their outputs rather than against a reference. These pairwise summaries are kept separate from reference agreement throughout the paper. Endpoint conservation is a consistency check, not proof of correct feature allocation.\n    88\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1014-L1030]]\n    89\t\n    90\t\\subsection{Compared Computations}\n    91\t\\paragraph{Prediction-only baselines.}\n    92\tThe library baseline uses \\texttt{shap.KernelExplainer} with the identity link, no L1 selection penalty, and the requested coalition cap $C$. Its split-cap comparator averages independent estimates using $\\lfloor C/2\\rfloor$ samples each. Both use the same serialized probability predictor, background, and queries as the structure-aware implementation. This comparator evaluates budget splitting, not the effect of doubling total resources. Endpoints, background expansion, and setup costs mean a coalition cap is not a literal wall-time or total prediction-row equality.\n    93\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L757-L772]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L871]]\n    95\t\n    96\t\\paragraph{Forest leaf-game computation.}\n    97\tFor every tree, the implementation intersects all splits on a feature along a root-to-leaf path. With query $x$ and background row $z_b$ fixed, a feature can satisfy the leaf constraint only when included, only when excluded, in both states, or in neither. A neither-state feature makes that leaf's game identically zero. Otherwise write $P$ for required inclusions and $Q$ for required exclusions, with sizes $p,q$; both-state features are dummy players. The leaf indicator is\n    98\t\\begin{equation}\n    99\t g(S)=\\mathbf{1}\\{P\\subseteq S,\\;Q\\cap S=\\varnothing\\}.\n   100\t\\end{equation}\n   101\tIntegrating the inclusion-probability marginal gives contribution coefficients\n   102\t\\begin{equation}\n   103\t \\phi_j(g)=\\begin{cases}\n   104\t   [p\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in P,\\\\\n   105\t   -[q\\binom{p+q}{p}]^{-1},&j\\in Q,\\\\\n   106\t   0,&j\\notin P\\cup Q.\n   107\t \\end{cases} \\label{eq:leaf}\n   108\t\\end{equation}\n   109\tMultiplying by leaf probabilities and averaging over trees and background rows exploits Shapley linearity. This avoids sampled coalition predictions for the forest branch. The implementation respects repeated-feature splits and the supplied predictor's float32 input and float64 threshold semantics; exactness refers to this finite, serialized game, up to floating-point arithmetic.\n   110\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/forest.py#L11-L81]]\n   111\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L720-L755]]\n   112\t\n   113\t\\paragraph{Probability-scale logistic integration.}\n   114\tFor one background row define $a=\\beta_0+\\beta^\\top z_b$ and $u_j=\\beta_j(x_j-z_{bj})$. The coalition probability is $\\sigma(a+\\sum_{j\\in S}u_j)$. The implemented sigmoid identity and inclusion integral yield\n   115\t\\begin{align}\n   116\t \\sigma(s)&=\\tfrac12+\\int_0^\\infty\\frac{\\sin(\\omega s)}{\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega,\\\\\n   117\t \\phi_j^{(b)}&=\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\left[e^{i\\omega a}(e^{i\\omega u_j}-1)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '117,197p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '117,197p'\"","aggregated_output":"   117\t \\phi_j^{(b)}&=\\int_0^\\infty\\!\\frac{\\operatorname{Im}\\!\\left[e^{i\\omega a}(e^{i\\omega u_j}-1)\n   118\t \\int_0^1\\prod_{k\\ne j}(1-t+t e^{i\\omega u_k})\\,dt\\right]}\n   119\t {\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\\,d\\omega. \\label{eq:logistic}\n   120\t\\end{align}\n   121\tThe inner polynomial has degree at most $d-1$, so $\\lceil d/2\\rceil$ Gauss--Legendre nodes integrate it exactly in exact arithmetic. Prefix/suffix products avoid division by possibly zero factors. The outer frequency integral is numerical, not an exact logistic SHAP solver.\n   122\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L12-L40]]\n   123\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L97-L140]]\n   124\t\n   125\tThe implementation truncates frequencies at 8, uses eight-node quadrature panels, and refines up to 2048 frequency nodes. It requires two consecutive mesh agreements and an endpoint residual within $10^{-8}$, with an analytic tail tolerance of $10^{-10}$. Mesh agreement does not bound every coordinate's discretization error. If any background row fails numerical checks, the whole logistic integration is rejected and the sampled fallback is retained. All supplied background rows are averaged; these checks do not select a favorable subset.\n   126\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L43-L94]]\n   127\t\n   128\t\\paragraph{Dispatch and sampled fallback.}\n   129\tForest structure is used directly. Other games are enumerated when the cap covers their nontrivial coalitions; otherwise logistic integration is considered. The fallback combines independent complete permutation paths with complement-paired kernel-distributed masks and an importance-corrected, endpoint-constrained regression. An internal signed top-feature separation test may select the path estimate instead. We have no recorded dispatch frequencies or test-acceptance coverage and make no empirical certification claim. The source ordering matters: high-cap exhaustive behavior must not be attributed to quadrature alone.\n   130\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L163-L197]]\n   131\t\n   132\t\\subsection{Design Rationale and Component Comparisons}\n   133\tThe design removes sampling where model structure permits same-game algebra, rather than changing what absent features mean. Forest leaf constraints expose a finite conjunction game; logistic additivity in the logit allows a probability-scale transform. We therefore expect the largest benefits where prediction-only estimates are inaccurate under the supplied cap. Deterministic computation is not itself the hypothesis test: an incorrect deterministic vector would be perfectly repeatable.\n   134\t\n   135\tTwo matched development comparisons examine the forest component with the nonforest computation retained, and logistic integration with the forest component retained. Readable source differences support this limited interpretation. These are narrower comparisons than a claim that every observed configuration difference isolates a single mathematical choice: the components also change access to model structure and computational work. There is no factorial study of quadrature safeguards, background size, or permutation-test acceptance.\n   136\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/sampled/reliability.py]]\n   137\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/forest/reliability.py]]\n   138\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py]]\n   139\t\n   140\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n   141\t\\paragraph{Data and frozen models.}\n   142\tThe final evaluation uses German Credit and ACSIncome-2024-RELSHIPP, each with logistic regression and a random forest. German retains 300 training, 100 validation, and 100 explanation records with 20 feature groups. ACS retains 20000, 6667, and 6667 records with 10 groups. Only the first 16 explanation records and first 16 training rows are used as queries and background in each condition; these are fixed across repeated calls, not randomly redrawn per explanation. Thus the split sizes should not be mistaken for the number of explained individuals.\n   143\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   144\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n   145\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L61-L155]]\n   146\t\n   147\tGerman records are partitioned by normalized predictor content into disjoint development and final subsets. ACS uses California for development and New York for final evaluation, after excluding cross-state predictor-content overlaps. The final ACS task replaces the historical \\texttt{RELP} field, absent from the retained release schema, with native \\texttt{RELSHIPP}; it is not bit-identical to historical ACSIncome. Other predictors, the adult population filter, and the income threshold are retained in the acquisition code. These choices define the evaluated population, not a population-representative survey analysis.\n   148\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json]]\n   149\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/capture.py#L87-L155]]\n   150\t\n   151\tCategorical vocabularies are fitted on training data and encoded ordinally; numerical missing values use training medians. Training means and standard deviations standardize all supplied groups. No one-hot masking study is included. Logistic models use \\texttt{lbfgs}, a 500-iteration cap and tolerance $10^{-7}$. Forests use 64 trees, maximum depth 6, and a single training thread. Model parameters and preprocessing remain frozen within a repeat comparison. Ordinal categories can impose artificial order; our results concern these fitted predictors, not optimal credit or income models.\n   152\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n   153\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L817-L848]]\n   154\t\n   155\t\\paragraph{Caps, seeds, and references.}\n   156\tFinal caps are 64, 256, and 1024. Each condition has six repeated structure-aware calls per cap for seeds 104729, 130363, and 155921. Each seed produces a fitted model/reference evaluation; queries and backgrounds are reused. ACS references enumerate all coalitions of the ten-feature game. German references average two independent library estimates, each with cap 4096. Their coordinatewise half-difference is retained as a reference-disagreement proxy, not a confidence interval or an error bound for their mean.\n   157\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   158\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L858]]\n   159\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1314-L1320]]\n   160\t\n   161\t\\paragraph{Reporting and uncertainty.}\n   162\tWe average metrics over query instances within each condition, then over the recorded seeds with equal weights. Repeated calls on the same query are not independent individuals. The stored 95\\% intervals resample the 16 query-level mean agreements using 1000 bootstrap resamples, after averaging caps and repeats within each fitted condition. They are neither pairwise-method confidence intervals nor uncertainty over retraining, background selection, or future deployment. Main plots show seed-level points and means, not invented standard errors. Library comparators have one recorded estimate per seed and cap; their within-model repeated-call consistency was not measured by this final protocol.\n   163\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1154-L1159]]\n   164\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   165\t\n   166\tThe completed final evaluation met its documented qualification checks. This establishes coverage and execution/control requirements, not semantic validity or a performance-based deployment threshold. No missing or failed empirical cell is used as evidence of inferiority. The full evaluation workload took 700.37 seconds of wall time, including references and comparators; candidate-only latency and total CPU-hours are unavailable. Development comparisons use two seeds, smaller query sets, and caps 64 and 256 and are reported separately.\n   167\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   168\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   169\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/wall_seconds]]\n   170\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n   171\t\n   172\t\\section{Results}\n   173\t\\subsection{Main Fixed-Game Comparison}\n   174\tFigure~\\ref{fig:final} compares computations within each dataset/model condition. At cap 64, mean structure-aware agreement is 0.9966 for German logistic and 0.9937 for German forest, against 0.9347 and 0.8806 for the one-shot library baseline. On ACS, both structure-aware conditions round to 1.0000 against the exhaustive reference. The corresponding one-shot library means are 0.9577 and 0.9636. These comparisons support improved numerical agreement in the retained low-cap settings, not a claim about new people, backgrounds, or classifiers.\n   175\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n   176\t\n   177\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   178\t\\centering\n   179\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/final_reference_comparison.pdf}\n   180\t\\caption{Final reference agreement by dataset, classifier, and coalition cap. Dots show the three fitted-condition seeds; lines connect cap-wise means, not a temporal trajectory. German references average two high-cap estimates; ACS references are exhaustive. The common zero-to-one scale makes the severe split-cap German failure visible; Table~\\ref{tab:main} resolves near-ceiling differences.}\n   181\t\\label{fig:final}\n   182\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   183\t\\end{figure}\n   184\t\n   185\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   186\t\\centering\n   187\t\\caption{Final cap-64 mean reference agreement and structure-aware reference sign agreement, averaged over seeds. The top-three reference overlap is 1 throughout for the structure-aware computation. Values rounded to 1.0000 are not claims of exact real-arithmetic equality.}\n   188\t\\label{tab:main}\n   189\t\\small\n   190\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n   191\t\\toprule\n   192\tCondition & One-shot & Split cap & Structure & Signs\\\\\n   193\t\\midrule\n   194\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   195\tGerman / logistic & 0.9347 & 0.3341 & 0.9966 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   196\tGerman / forest & 0.8806 & 0.2995 & 0.9937 & 0.9979 \\\\\n   197\tACS / logistic & 0.9577 & 0.9349 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 \\\\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '198,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '198,275p'\"","aggregated_output":"   198\tACS / forest & 0.9636 & 0.9378 & 1.0000 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   199\t\\bottomrule\n   200\t\\end{tabular}\n   201\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n   202\t\\end{table}\n   203\t\n   204\tIncreasing the library cap improves mean reference agreement in all four final conditions. At cap 1024, its German logistic and forest means reach 0.9889 and 0.9802, while both ACS means round to 1.0000. The implemented exhaustive dispatch can also apply at this ACS cap. The evidence therefore does not support saying that structure-aware computation is necessary for high agreement in every measured setting. Nor does an unchanged structure-aware curve establish budget efficiency: parameter access allows computations that are not charged as coalition predictions.\n   205\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n   206\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py#L173-L184]]\n   207\t\n   208\tThe recorded aggregate normalized reference agreement is 0.9975639. We do not use the evaluator's mixed sign summary as if it were pure reference agreement: directly pooling the recorded reference-comparison cells gives sign agreement 0.9994792. Every structure-aware top-three set agrees with its reference, yet near-perfect membership and signs do not imply identical attribution magnitudes. Supplementary tables preserve the condition- and seed-level evidence instead of collapsing reference quality into a single success label.\n   209\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/reference_fidelity]]\n   210\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/reference_sign_mean]]\n   211\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   212\t\n   213\t\\subsection{Repeat Consistency Is Stronger Than Observed Reference Equality}\n   214\tAll retained structure-aware repeat-pair top-three and sign agreements equal 1. Inspecting the stored vectors also yields a maximum coordinate range across repeats of zero. This is consistent with deterministic dispatch in these conditions, but dispatch counts are not recorded. It does not show that an arbitrary fallback call will be deterministic or accurately attributed. There are only six calls per cap on a small fixed query set, and no population-level zero-failure guarantee follows.\n   215\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   216\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/max_repeat_vector_difference]]\n   217\t\n   218\tGerman provides a useful separation: identical repeated outputs still differ from the approximate reference. The recorded query-bootstrap interval for German logistic at seed 104729 is $[0.99618,0.99709]$; the analogous forest interval is $[0.99292,0.99442]$. These intervals describe query heterogeneity conditional on the recorded reference, not uncertainty about the true Shapley vector. The mean absolute coordinate half-differences between German reference replicates are approximately $1.05\\times10^{-4}$ for logistic and $1.20\\times10^{-4}$ for forests. We cannot attribute all residual estimate-to-reference distance to either side from that proxy alone.\n   219\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/observations/fidelity_instance_bootstrap_ci95]]\n   220\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/1/observations/fidelity_instance_bootstrap_ci95]]\n   221\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/reference_uncertainty]]\n   222\t\n   223\tACS has a stronger computational interpretation because its reference exhausts the specified finite game. Agreement there supports numerical fidelity to that game, without validating background choice or impossible hybrid records. The observed endpoint residual averages $1.28\\times10^{-11}$; recorded control pass rate is 1, failed-call fraction is zero, and replay error is zero. These are narrow numerical and operational checks, not independent evidence that feature attributions are good reasons for decisions.\n   224\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/efficiency_error]]\n   225\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate]]\n   226\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/failed_fraction]]\n   227\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/replay_error]]\n   228\t\n   229\t\\subsection{Budget Splitting and Component Sensitivity}\n   230\tAveraging is not automatically helpful under a fixed total cap. At cap 64 the split-cap German logistic mean is 0.3341 and the forest mean is 0.2995, substantially below the one-shot means. By cap 256 those split-cap means recover to 0.9699 and 0.9448. The result establishes a failure of this particular low-cap split, not a general failure of averaging independent estimators. The retained diagnostics do not identify a unique numerical cause; reduced fit information is a plausible interpretation, not a measured mechanism.\n   231\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/final]]\n   232\t\n   233\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   234\t\\centering\n   235\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/component_comparison.pdf}\n   236\t\\caption{Matched component comparisons on development data only. Left: forest computation with and without the exact leaf-game component. Right: logistic computation with and without integration. Each category shows the two recorded seed values and a mean tick; the vertical axis is restricted to $[0.8,1]$. Ger/ACS denote dataset and labels underneath give coalition caps. The unchanged model family is retained in each comparison; structural access and work still differ.}\n   237\t\\label{fig:components}\n   238\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n   239\t\\end{figure}\n   240\t\n   241\tDevelopment comparisons in Figure~\\ref{fig:components} support family-localized changes. At cap 64, German forest reference agreement changes from 0.858--0.870 without the leaf-game component to 0.9924--0.9929 with it. German logistic changes from 0.946--0.951 without integration to approximately 0.9963 with integration. In both ACS comparisons, the structure-aware outputs agree with exhaustive references to the displayed precision. These are matched configuration comparisons, not evidence that an untested safeguard or formula alone caused every gain.\n   242\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n   243\t\n   244\tThe sampled development estimates also show nonmonotone cap behavior: ACS forest mean agreement without its structure component falls from approximately 0.9674 at cap 64 to 0.9512 at cap 256. German logistic without integration similarly falls from about 0.9488 to 0.9137. This concerns the custom sampled fallback, not the final library curve. More sampled coalitions need not improve an individual finite estimate, and the recorded comparisons do not isolate whether sampling mix, fit conditioning, or another interaction explains the pattern. No missing ablation is treated as a measured failure.\n   245\t% @evidence[[analysis_summary.json#/development]]\n   246\t\n   247\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n   248\t\\paragraph{What a single explanation supports.}\n   249\tFor these retained games, a single structure-aware output is empirically representative of its repeated-call outputs and closely matches the available reference. This is a statement about computation. A deployment policy still needs a tolerance on magnitude error, ranking errors, and sign errors that reflects its use case. We did not evaluate such a policy, elicited human requirements, or a threshold determining when a vector is sufficiently reliable to justify a decision. In particular, high mean agreement can hide rare query-specific behavior and the normalization in Equation~\\ref{eq:agreement} does not directly encode decision harm.\n   250\t\n   251\t\\paragraph{Reference and dependence limits.}\n   252\tThe German reference's own approximation prevents assigning all remaining discrepancy to the tested method. Exhaustive ACS attribution removes that specific ambiguity, not the choice of game. The small fixed background, ordinal feature representation, and replacement of missing features can create unrealistic hybrids. There is no conditional-game, background-shift, refitting-stability, adversarial, or user study. Fixed queries are reused across seeds, so pooled cells and the many repeat pairs do not create a large independent sample. Bootstrap intervals do not account for shared reference bias.\n   253\t\n   254\t\\paragraph{Access, selection, and external validity.}\n   255\tStructure-aware computation uses more information than a prediction-only service may expose. We lack candidate-only timing, branch counts, and equal-time comparisons. It is therefore inappropriate to translate the cap-wise agreement advantage into an unrestricted speed or cost advantage. Development selection introduces adaptivity; the held-out evaluation narrows but does not eliminate all selection concerns. The source protocol supplies only two tabular datasets and two model families, with few queries. Geographic separation for ACS is not a claim of cross-task generalization to medicine, other years, or neural models. Source inspection supports the released computational mechanisms, while unexecuted independent validation scripts are not counted as additional experimental evidence.\n   256\t\n   257\t\\paragraph{Scope of the contribution.}\n   258\tThe strongest supported finding is conditional: structural access can produce repeatable, reference-consistent computations of the chosen finite game under the supplied caps. This does not establish universal reliability of one-shot SHAP, superiority over unmeasured estimators, or validity of its use in high-risk decisions. The negative split-cap result and the unresolved German reference discrepancy are part of that finding, not exceptions to remove from the report.\n   259\t\n   260\t\\section{Conclusion}\n   261\tComputational reliability of a feature attribution should distinguish repeating an answer from computing the intended answer. For the frozen German Credit and adapted ACS income models studied here, forest leaf-game attribution and probability-scale logistic integration yield identical observed repeated-call vectors and high agreement with their respective references. The library baseline approaches exhaustive ACS agreement at the largest cap, while dividing a small cap between two estimates can be substantially worse than one library call. Thus neither repeated execution nor averaging supplies a universal remedy.\n   262\t\n   263\tThe strength of the conclusion depends on what is held fixed and what is known. Exhaustive finite-game references support a sharper computational interpretation than approximate references. Structural access, fixed backgrounds, and a small reused query set prevent equal-time or broad deployment claims. A reliable numerical solution is one prerequisite for using an attribution, not evidence that the background, feature semantics, classifier, or downstream decision is justified. Future assessments need application-specific tolerances, broader query coverage, reference diagnostics, and matched cost accounting before making that stronger claim.\n   264\t\n   265\t\\clearpage\n   266\t\\bibliography{references}\n   267\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n   268\t\\clearpage\n   269\t\\appendix\n   270\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n   271\t\n   272\t\\subsection{Fixed-Game Construction, Data Provenance, and Models}\n   273\tThe public acquisition and split implementation is included as\n   274\t\\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluation/capture.py}; data URLs, release digests, licenses, and the schema adapter are in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/evaluation/data\\_provenance.json}. German Credit originates from UCI DOI 10.24432/C5NC77 under CC BY 4.0. ACS records originate from the 2024 one-year California and New York PUMS releases. The folktables code is MIT-licensed; the Census materials are public government data with site terms archived by the original acquisition. No separate SPDX data license is inferred.\n   275\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/data_provenance.json]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
