{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '348,428p'; jq -r '.claims | to_entries[] | select(.key<30 or .key>=70) | \\\"\\\\(.key): \\\\(.value.claim)\\\"' claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"   348\t130363 & German / forest & 256 & 0.9567 & 0.9421 & 0.9935 \\\\\n   349\t130363 & German / forest & 1024 & 0.9787 & 0.9779 & 0.9935 \\\\\n   350\t130363 & ACS / logistic & 64 & 0.9584 & 0.9397 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   351\t130363 & ACS / logistic & 256 & 0.9834 & 0.9809 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   352\t130363 & ACS / logistic & 1024 & 1.0000 & 0.9928 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   353\t130363 & ACS / forest & 64 & 0.9666 & 0.9456 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   354\t130363 & ACS / forest & 256 & 0.9877 & 0.9862 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   355\t130363 & ACS / forest & 1024 & 1.0000 & 0.9940 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   356\t155921 & German / logistic & 64 & 0.9388 & 0.2977 & 0.9964 \\\\\n   357\t155921 & German / logistic & 256 & 0.9761 & 0.9712 & 0.9964 \\\\\n   358\t155921 & German / logistic & 1024 & 0.9894 & 0.9871 & 0.9964 \\\\\n   359\t155921 & German / forest & 64 & 0.8894 & 0.3178 & 0.9939 \\\\\n   360\t155921 & German / forest & 256 & 0.9533 & 0.9470 & 0.9939 \\\\\n   361\t155921 & German / forest & 1024 & 0.9813 & 0.9790 & 0.9939 \\\\\n   362\t155921 & ACS / logistic & 64 & 0.9544 & 0.9288 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   363\t155921 & ACS / logistic & 256 & 0.9838 & 0.9797 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   364\t155921 & ACS / logistic & 1024 & 1.0000 & 0.9939 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   365\t155921 & ACS / forest & 64 & 0.9639 & 0.9328 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   366\t155921 & ACS / forest & 256 & 0.9860 & 0.9818 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   367\t155921 & ACS / forest & 1024 & 1.0000 & 0.9945 & 1.0000 \\\\\n   368\t\\bottomrule\n   369\t\\end{longtable}\n   370\t\n   371\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   372\t\\centering\n   373\t\\caption{Stored 95\\% instance-bootstrap intervals for structure-aware reference agreement, conditional on each fitted model and reference. ACS values rounding to one have residuals below the displayed resolution.}\n   374\t\\label{tab:intervals}\n   375\t\\small\n   376\t\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n   377\t\\toprule Seed & Condition & Interval\\\\\\midrule\n   378\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   379\t104729 & German / logistic & [0.9961794823, 0.9970868158] \\\\\n   380\t104729 & German / forest & [0.9929222303, 0.9944210303] \\\\\n   381\t104729 & ACS / logistic & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   382\t104729 & ACS / forest & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   383\t130363 & German / logistic & [0.9960508216, 0.9970566319] \\\\\n   384\t130363 & German / forest & [0.9924407908, 0.9945685087] \\\\\n   385\t130363 & ACS / logistic & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   386\t130363 & ACS / forest & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   387\t155921 & German / logistic & [0.9959843487, 0.9968429357] \\\\\n   388\t155921 & German / forest & [0.9928739317, 0.9947199151] \\\\\n   389\t155921 & ACS / logistic & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   390\t155921 & ACS / forest & [1.0000000000, 1.0000000000] \\\\\n   391\t\\bottomrule\n   392\t\\end{tabular}\n   393\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   394\t\\end{table}\n   395\t\n   396\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   397\t\\centering\n   398\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/reference_diagnostics.pdf}\n   399\t\\caption{German reference diagnostics. Each dot is a retained query/seed pair; color distinguishes seeds. The horizontal coordinate is the L1 norm of the two reference estimates' coordinatewise half-difference. The vertical coordinate is structure-aware distance to their mean at the smallest cap and first repeat. The dashed line marks equality of these descriptive distances, not a calibrated uncertainty boundary.}\n   400\t\\label{fig:reference}\n   401\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   402\t\\end{figure}\n   403\t\n   404\tFigure~\\ref{fig:reference} makes the reference ambiguity explicit. The reference half-difference is a measure of disagreement between two numerical estimates, not an estimate of all systematic error. A point below or above the diagonal is not evidence for or against a formal coverage statement. It is inappropriate to subtract this proxy from the observed error to obtain a supposedly debiased accuracy metric.\n   405\t\n   406\t\\subsection{Component Comparison Details}\n   407\tDevelopment comparisons use seeds 1729 and 2718, eight queries per condition, four repeated calls, and coalition caps 64 and 256. They share the same within-condition game. Adding forest computation leaves the nonforest dispatch unchanged. Adding logistic integration retains the forest implementation and inserts integration before the existing sampled computation; the generic exhaustive branch remains ahead of it. The matched snapshots support model-family localization, but not a claim about each internal safeguard or an unmeasured estimator.\n   408\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n   409\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/forest/reliability.py]]\n   410\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/reliability.py]]\n   411\t\n   412\tNo controlled comparison removes only the frequency cutoff, only the endpoint check, or only background averaging. The nonmonotone sampled results are retained rather than relabeled as missing or invalid observations. Conversely, the presence of written but unexecuted independent validation routines cannot establish empirical failure or success. Development data were used to select the retained computation, so these comparisons are supporting evidence, separate from the final evaluation.\n   413\t\n   414\t\\subsection{Reproducibility and Compute Accounting}\n   415\tThe recorded environment specification is included as\n   416\t\\path{source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt}. The numerical stack includes NumPy 2.1.3 \\citep{Harris2020}, SciPy 1.15.3 \\citep{Virtanen2020}, scikit-learn 1.6.1 \\citep{Pedregosa2011}, SHAP 0.48.0, pandas 2.2.3 \\citep{McKinney2010}, and folktables 0.0.12. These are the recorded requirements, not a claim that every dependency was independently reinstalled during writing. Figure rendering uses Matplotlib \\citep{Hunter2007}; writing-time rendering tools are isolated outside the manuscript workspace.\n   417\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n   418\t\n   419\t\\texttt{REPRODUCIBILITY.md} records offline evaluation and data-recapture commands, source locations, and rendering steps. To regenerate quantitative figures and tables from the retained registry, run \\texttt{python build\\_artifacts.py} in an environment with NumPy, Matplotlib, and Pillow. This script generates Figures~\\ref{fig:final}, \\ref{fig:components}, and \\ref{fig:reference}, and all numerical table fragments. It does not rerun training or explanations. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a separate conceptual rendering; its placeholder is self-guarded and is not an experimental result. Adjacent provenance sidecars link derived artifacts to retained episode arrays.\n   420\t\n   421\tThe final evaluation's 700.37-second wall total includes references, library comparators, training, and execution overhead; it is not per-query latency or CPU usage. The study does not have an equal-wall-time or total-model-evaluation benchmark for structure-aware computation. Reference prediction-row counters and baseline timings are present in the retained observations, but do not recover a complete candidate cost ledger. The stated source-task CPU allowance is not measured consumption and is not reported as such.\n   422\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/wall_seconds]]\n   423\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   424\t\n   425\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n   426\tLLM-based assistants were used for research design proposals, estimator code preparation, inspection of source and evaluation artifacts, reference-query planning, manuscript framing and drafting, derivation of descriptive tables and plotting code, and grounding/format checks. The retained evaluations were executed by the evaluation workflow, not by prose generation. Written independent validation routines without recorded executions are explicitly distinguished from measured evidence. No human-subject judgments, human review outcomes, or manual verification by domain experts are claimed. Literature use here is limited to directly retrieved known primary bibliographic records and available abstracts; no citation-network traversal or search for the prohibited study was used in manuscript preparation. Reference discovery noise was removed rather than treated as read or relevant literature.\n   427\t\n   428\t\\end{document}\n0: Final qualification independently reports completed/qualified/true. This concerns documented integrity, coverage, execution and controls, not deployment validity.\n1: Aggregate final normalized reference agreement is 0.9975639445869278, rounded to 0.99756 in the abstract.\n2: All final structure-aware reference top-three overlaps and repeat-pair top-three/sign agreements equal one; these are separate assessments.\n3: Stored vectors have zero maximum coordinate range across repeats within each fixed cap/model/query.\n4: Pure reference sign agreement is 0.9994791667, not the mixed evaluator sign metric.\n5: Mean normalized endpoint residual is 1.27919753412707e-11, control pass rate one, failed-call fraction zero and replay error zero.\n6: The recorded full workload takes 700.3707705293782 wall seconds, rounded to 700.37, including references, comparators, training and overhead; candidate-only latency is unavailable.\n7: Four final dataset/model conditions are German logistic, German forest, ACS logistic and ACS forest; no additional task list or downstream study is present.\n8: Final seeds are 104729, 130363 and 155921; caps 64/256/1024; six repeats per cap; sixteen fixed queries and training-background rows per fitted condition.\n9: Final German retained splits are 300/100/100 records and twenty groups; ACS 20000/6667/6667 and ten groups. These are protocol counts, not independent explained-sample counts.\n10: German uses original class code 2. ACS uses 2024 PUMS, native RELSHIPP without a RELP crosswalk, the retained adult filter and PINCP>50000 target.\n11: German development/final content groups are disjoint. ACS uses California development and New York final data after excluding raw predictor-content overlaps.\n12: Training-fitted ordinal vocabularies use unseen code -1; numeric medians and standardization are training fitted; queries/background use fixed ordering.\n13: Logistic regression uses lbfgs, max_iter 500 and tolerance 1e-7. Forests use sixty-four trees, depth six and n_jobs one.\n14: ACS references enumerate ten-feature finite games; German references average two independent cap-4096 library estimates. Their half-difference is a proxy, not a calibrated interval.\n15: Stored 95-percent intervals use 1000 bootstrap resamples of sixteen query-level means after cap/repeat averaging, conditional on one fitted model/reference.\n16: The normalized L1 agreement floor is 0.001; signs at magnitude at most 1e-8 are zero; top-three ties follow coordinate order. Endpoint residual is capped at one.\n17: Library KernelExplainer uses identity link, l1_reg zero and nsamples equal to cap. Two independent split-cap calls each use floor(cap/2).\n18: Forest attribution uses signed leaf-game beta-integral coefficients over all trees/background rows, intersecting repeated splits and retaining float32 predictor-input semantics.\n19: Probability-scale logistic integration uses ceil(d/2) inclusion nodes, eight-node frequency panels, cutoff eight and at most 2048 frequency nodes; tail tolerance 1e-10 and mesh/efficiency tolerances 1e-8.\n20: Two consecutive mesh agreements are required. Failure for any background row rejects integration; frequency-tail control is not a rigorous discretization bound.\n21: Dispatch uses forest structure directly, then generic enumeration when cap covers 2^d-2 nontrivial coalitions before logistic integration; branch counts are unrecorded.\n22: The sampled fallback combines independent complete paths and importance-corrected constrained regression. Alpha 0.05 separation is used only with at least two paths; acceptance coverage is absent.\n23: The release contains independent validation routines without retained executions. They are not additional empirical controls.\n24: Recorded dependencies include NumPy 2.1.3, SciPy 1.15.3, scikit-learn 1.6.1, SHAP 0.48.0, pandas 2.2.3 and folktables 0.0.12; a fresh full-lock installation was not tested.\n25: German reference mean absolute coordinate half-differences are about 1.05e-4 logistic and 1.20e-4 forest; no error decomposition follows.\n26: Seed-104729 query-bootstrap intervals round to [0.99618,0.99709] German logistic and [0.99292,0.99442] German forest.\n27: Final cap-64 structure agreement is 0.9966/0.9937 German logistic/forest, versus library 0.9347/0.8806; ACS structure rounds to one versus library 0.9577/0.9636.\n28: At cap 1024 final library means are 0.9889/0.9802 German logistic/forest and round to one for both ACS cases. This does not establish necessity of structure-aware access.\n29: Split-cap German means at cap 64 are 0.3341/0.2995, recovering to 0.9699/0.9448 at cap 256; recorded diagnostics do not isolate a cause.\n70: Development figure: configuration 0, seed 1729, german_logistic, cap 64: agreement 0.9513221205.\n71: Development figure: configuration 0, seed 1729, german_logistic, cap 256: agreement 0.9104719318.\n72: Development figure: configuration 0, seed 1729, german_forest, cap 64: agreement 0.8699825805.\n73: Development figure: configuration 0, seed 1729, german_forest, cap 256: agreement 0.8790287035.\n74: Development figure: configuration 0, seed 1729, acs_logistic, cap 64: agreem