{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq '.references | type, (if type==\\\"array\\\" then .[0] else to_entries[0] end)' references_provenance.json; jq '.evidence_sources.investigation | type, keys' paper_data.json; cat figure_manifest.json; sed -n '289,301p' paper.tex; sed -n '150,160p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\"object\"\n[\n  \"path\",\n  \"status\"\n]\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"fallback_reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n      \"figure_id\": \"fixed_game_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"generator\": null,\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/fixed_game_overview.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/fixed_game_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"final_reference_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 14.545454545454547,\n        \"legend\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"ticks\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"title\": 14.545454545454547\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/final_reference_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/final_reference_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\",\n        \"final.metric.reference_fidelity\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        4,\n        4\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"component_comparison\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 14.545454545454547,\n        \"legend\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"ticks\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"title\": 14.545454545454547\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/component_comparison.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/component_comparison.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        2\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"result.top_program_01\",\n        \"result.top_program_02\",\n        \"result.top_program_03\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        0,\n        0\n      ],\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9.0,\n      \"figure_id\": \"reference_diagnostics\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"scatter\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 14.545454545454547,\n        \"legend\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"ticks\": 13.090909090909092,\n        \"title\": 14.545454545454547\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 29,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/reference_diagnostics.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/reference_diagnostics.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\\paragraph{Leaf contributions.}\nFor a viable leaf, the expected indicator under independent feature inclusion with probability $t$ is $t^p(1-t)^q$. Including a required-positive feature changes this by $t^{p-1}(1-t)^q$, while including a required-negative feature changes it by $-t^p(1-t)^{q-1}$. Integrating each marginal from zero to one yields Equation~\\ref{eq:leaf}. Features not constrained by that leaf contribute zero. A constant leaf contributes no feature attribution, though it contributes to the empty/full value. Repeated splits are intersected into lower-exclusive and upper-inclusive bounds; contradictory paths are removed. The exact finite-game claim depends on matching these traversal semantics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/forest.py#L11-L81]]\n\n\\paragraph{Frequency truncation is not discretization certification.}\nIn Equation~\\ref{eq:logistic}, the phase difference has magnitude at most two and every inclusion factor has magnitude at most one. Thus the omitted tail beyond frequency $W$ is bounded coordinatewise by\n\\begin{equation}\n 2\\int_W^\\infty\\frac{d\\omega}{\\sinh(\\pi\\omega)}\n =-\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\log\\tanh\\!\\left(\\frac{\\pi W}{2}\\right).\n\\end{equation}\nThis is the source implementation's frequency-tail bound. It does not bound mesh discretization error on the retained interval. The implementation uses agreement under refinement and endpoint conservation as additional numerical diagnostics, rejects nonfinite arithmetic or excessive phase ranges, and averages all background rows only after successful integration. Prefix/suffix products keep zero mixture factors well-defined. The wrapper catches floating-point overflow and invalid arithmetic and returns rejection without consuming the sampling generator.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/logistic_fourier.py#L12-L140]]\n\n\nCategorical vocabularies are fitted on training data and encoded ordinally; numerical missing values use training medians. Training means and standard deviations standardize all supplied groups. No one-hot masking study is included. Logistic models use \\texttt{lbfgs}, a 500-iteration cap and tolerance $10^{-7}$. Forests use 64 trees, maximum depth 6, and a single training thread. Model parameters and preprocessing remain frozen within a repeat comparison. Ordinal categories can impose artificial order; our results concern these fitted predictors, not optimal credit or income models.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L687-L718]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L817-L848]]\n\n\\paragraph{Caps, seeds, and references.}\nFinal caps are 64, 256, and 1024. Each condition has six repeated structure-aware calls per cap for seeds 104729, 130363, and 155921. Each seed produces a fitted model/reference evaluation; queries and backgrounds are reused. ACS references enumerate all coalitions of the ten-feature game. German references average two independent library estimates, each with cap 4096. Their coordinatewise half-difference is retained as a reference-disagreement proxy, not a confidence interval or an error bound for their mean.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L848-L858]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1314-L1320]]\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
